WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:03.510
Film Courage: Where does the creation of a
great story begin?

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.810
Scott Myers, Screenwriter/Creator of Gointothestory.blcklst.com/Professor
at DePaul University: I think it varies from

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.790
story to story, from writer to writer.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:13.120
You know I’ve interviewed so many of them.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.750
Some of them start with a theme.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.950
I don’t do that.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:23.500
A central theme of a story will emerge over
time.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.289
But some people start off with a thematic
point of view.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:33.030
A lot of people start (that I’ve interviewed)
and this is more where I’m at start off

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:43.250
with a character or characters and I think
that works because if the character is interesting

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:49.000
and you can mine that then that’s where
people can find those points of connection.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.930
Some start off with a story concept.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.719
I do this thing on my blog [Gointothestory.blcklst.com].

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:57.930
I’ve done it now ten straight years called
The Story Idea Each Day For A Month.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:07.580
So every April (for 30 days) I collect stories
along the way in my computer and then I just

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.430
run one each day and I say “Okay, so here’s
the basis of this news article.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.040
What would we do with this if this were a
movie?”

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.360
And get people to think about this.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.580
I’d say “What would we do if this were
a drama?

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.200
What if we did this as a thriller?”

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.430
Like I just heard this on the way over.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.380
This is amazing.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:29.259
So a guy is at Dallas Love Field today (a
passenger, waiting to take off) when there’s

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:36.200
this hubbub there and as it turns out, they’re
bring back the body of the remains of a guy

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:44.170
who fought for the United States in Vietnam
and they’ve recently discovered his remains.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:54.170
His son was 5 when he last saw his father
at that base leaving to go to Vietnam.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:01.630
The pilot of the plane flying his father’s
remains to Dallas is his son.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.719
Isn’t that amazing?

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:14.970
So they had all those people who do the luggage
and they created this line and they had a

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.000
water tribute.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.110
They were shooting with a fire hose and stuff.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.130
They had military guys there.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:25.690
Everybody was pressed up against the windows
because this word had spread that this was

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.890
happening and he said it was the eeriest thing.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.140
Nobody’s phone, nobody talking, no “click-click-click”
of the luggage and everybody is just standing

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.140
still watching this reverent moment happen.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.920
So I’d say things like the news articles...you
know I don’t know if that’s a great story

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.810
or not but it’s a wonderful moment.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.920
Maybe that’s an opportunity to be a story.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.700
So I think it can happen from anywhere, be
open to it.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.970
The magic words “What If?”

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.190
Right?

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.250
Mark Norman’s son calls him up and says
“What if Shakespeare had writer’s block?”

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:07.480
And that was the inspiration for SHAKESPEARE
IN LOVE.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:16.290
Capt. Hook written by James V. Hart came about
in a breakfast conversation where his son

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.140
said “What if Peter Pan grew up?”

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.980
So I think concepts are big.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.420
I think they are really important now maybe.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.040
Maybe as important as they were...the ‘High
Concept’ thing that we talked about in the

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.860
80’s and 90’s so much...because of this
whole idea that unless you’ve got some sort

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.650
of pre-existing IP [intellectual property],
Hollywood is scared to green-light that.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.590
So it takes a really, really strong concept,
a strong story concept.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:46.980
So I’d say theme, characters, story concept.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.400
I love the Coen Brothers.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.120
The way that MILLER’S CROSSING started was
an image of a hat being tossed across a creek

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.210
in the woods.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.940
That is literally where that started.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:00.410
So whatever it is, find if you are passionate
about it, and if you’re passionate about

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.400
it then write the heck out of it.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.670
Film Courage: I’m sorry, why have screenplays
changed in terms of reading one from 20 years

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:12.290
ago, from one 2 years ago?

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.670
Are thing written in a briefer manner?

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.000
Scott: Yes.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.160
Now this is where one area I feel pretty confident
that I understand pretty well.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.940
I used to teach a class called The History
of American Screenwriting, which was created

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.460
by my colleague down at the University of
North Carolina (Dana Cohen) and I taught that

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.610
class I think 4 times.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:38.600
It was really a brilliant class because it
would go decade by decade starting with like

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:44.640
the 1890’s, every week was like a different
decade.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.380
And so you are starting the evolution of screenwriting
and filmmaking (particularly in the United

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:56.020
States) and the role of the screenwriter in
the process (particularly in Hollywood).

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.430
One of the things that we did in that class
was we looked at screenplay structure and

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.040
format and style as it evolved over time.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:12.020
In fact the term screenplay, didn’t really
come into existence until the 30’s and 40’s.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.580
Before then it was basically just a shot list.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:20.130
And then it became known as a continuity during
the silent film era.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.780
But then one talkies came along then Hollywood
started to bring in all of these playwrights

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.090
to do the dialogue right?

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.300
So then it became a screen play (two words).

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.500
So if you look at movies in the 30’s and
even the 40’s you’ll often time see that

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:41.020
credit being screen play (a screen version
of a play).

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:46.520
Then eventually screenplays kind of became
more of their own thing and we joined them

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.850
together as screenplay.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:57.580
I’ve studied this quite comprehensively
and I know for a fact that it’s always evolving

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:02.500
so that always cracked me up when people say
“Well you can’t do this.”

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.450
Or “You have to do that.”

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:08.000
It’s always evolving, it’s constantly
evolving.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:17.100
So for example if you look at scripts from…there
were no such thing as selling scripts or spec

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.191
scripts back in the 40’s, 50’s and 60’s,
it was all for hire (well 30’s, 40’s and

00:06:23.191 --> 00:06:24.191
50’s).

00:06:24.191 --> 00:06:29.140
So all of these things were filled with shots
(close-up, zoom in, all of that stuff).

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:34.210
What happened once the spec script market
evolved was the director said “We don’t

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.380
want you to tell us how to direct the movie.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:37.380
We’re directors.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.380
We’re going to do that.”

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.460
So we pulled out that camera jargon and directing
lingo.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.860
And so that led to the whole We See, We Hear,
We Move.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.590
Then that started to get a bit tiresome and
so what we decided to so is just describe

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:53.150
what’s happening.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:56.590
So then when I first broke in it was like
well every paragraph you should have no more

00:06:56.590 --> 00:07:01.460
than five lines as opposed to like these big
blocks of 15, 20, 30 lines because it’s

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.590
much easier to read.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:06.610
Script readers are in effect the threshold
guardians of Hollywood and we try to make

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:10.090
it as easy for them to read as possible.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:16.139
Nowadays I think the general feeling (and
this is not a rule) but the general feeling

00:07:16.139 --> 00:07:20.060
is a paragraph should not be longer than three
lines.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:24.340
But a better way to think about it is not
like that regimented thing, the better way

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:30.670
to think about it is each line of scene description,
each paragraph of scene description, that’s

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.870
a way of indicating a camera shot.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:37.230
You are not saying it’s a camera shot but
if you think about the scene as you’re writing

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.419
it and you say “Okay that’s a close-up.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:44.290
I see this as a wide shot and I see this as
a medium shot.”

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:48.830
Now you’re not saying this in the script
but you’re breaking up each paragraph to

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.919
suggest that that’s a camera shot.

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:58.110
That’s great way to not only direct the
action on the page but also ensure that your

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.580
paragraphs aren’t that long.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.840
You’re breaking them up into one, two or
three lines.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:07.960
So scripts today, that why it’s important
to read the older scripts but it’s really

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:13.770
important to read the scripts that come out
nowadays because the styles are constantly

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:14.770
changing.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.560
We’re seeing something happen right now
and I don’t know if we’re going to continue

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.760
to see it go this way but including images
in scripts.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:29.300
Like if you read the script that Brian Woods
and Scott Beck did for A QUIET PLACE, it has

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.010
an image of the monopoly board.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:36.631
It’s got an image comparing the Stature
of Liberty and then The Eiffel Tower and then

00:08:36.631 --> 00:08:40.070
this big tower that’s out in the field there
just so you get a sense of it.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:43.229
They change font sizes.

00:08:43.229 --> 00:08:47.340
One page is like a word on it or even a letter.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:53.620
So it’s much more visually graphic and I
guess it makes sense because movies are primarily

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.860
a visual medium.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.670
Some people may resist that as being kind
of a cheat but we’re starting to see some

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.310
of that now and I don’t think that this
is necessarily wrong.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:11.150
It could be that this is what screenplays
become, they do include images.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:14.010
And you could certainly make an argument that
“Why not?”

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.270
Because again it’s a visual medium.

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:22.910
So it’s constantly in a state of change
which is why if anybody tells you “You can’t

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:23.910
do this.”

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.860
Or “You have to do that.”

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.870
Then you know you’re not talking to the
right people.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:33.200
You need to read script by professional screenwriters
and see what they do and realize that you

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.830
have a lot of latitude to tell the story the
best way it needs to be told.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:42.940
Film Courage: And maybe that’s why emojis
and things like that have seeped into writing?

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.490
I don’t know if you’ve received papers
with an emoji here or there?

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.250
But it’s become part of communication.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.070
Scott: You see them in scripts because of
texting now.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:57.120
We didn’t have texting until 15 years ago
or what not right?

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.510
So some people (my university students) will
try to do that as dialogue.

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:04.220
Well it’s not really dialogue, it is its
own thing.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:11.210
So the typical thing now is to do left margin
just like you’re doing action right?

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:16.401
And you’re doing the name of the character
and colon and the little texting thing and

00:10:16.401 --> 00:10:18.160
it can have an emoji in it.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.770
And then you do a shift - enter [on the keyboard].

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:24.830
You don’t two lines space, you do shift
- enter (so it’s right under there), shift

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:26.550
- enter - the next text.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:31.839
And that’s how most people that I know handling
texting.

00:10:31.839 --> 00:10:33.740
But yes emojis would be there.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:34.740
Who knows?

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:38.149
In ten years we will be doing something different
and that’s going to be reflect in screenplays.

00:10:38.149 --> 00:10:40.610
Film Courage: Some kind of Matrix thing.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:41.579
Scott: Yes.

